content:
### Rationale
I could not find a structural S-band change that works on the A100 and is also likely to carry over to the B200. Giving each 256-thread CTA 2 tiles was 4–6% faster at 128/131 tokens, but 14% slower at 256 tokens with 4 tiles. That A100 gain is a wave effect: the parent runs in 1.8 waves on 108 SMs but in a single wave on the B200's 148. That idea is also the same as the proposed r4-cute-ldg256-sw2-disp, so I dropped it. Instead the candidate isolates the one fixed-cost term r4-cute leaves alone: dirty-L2 traffic on the load side. For B·S ≤ 600 it uses the load policy CAKE kept after paired cold-L2 tests on the B200: L2 evict_first on x loads, and weights through `ld.global.nc`. Stores stay evict_last. Larger sizes run the parent unchanged. On the A100 it changes nothing (S +0.3%, M/L 0%), so any B200 effect is B200-only and probably small.

